        "id": "p17-q12-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Explain why $A\\cap B$ is equal to the elements of set $A$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$A$ is a subset of $B$, so every element of $A$ is in $B$.",
          "worked": "The diagram shows $A\\subset B$. Every element of $A$ is also in $B$, so $A\\cap B=A$.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      },
      {
        "id": "p17-q12-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Give an example of two possible sets $A$ and $B$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "For example, $A=\\{\\text{multiples of }4\\}$ and $B=\\{\\text{even numbers}\\}$.",
          "worked": "Choose $A$ as the multiples of $4$ and $B$ as the even numbers. Every multiple of $4$ is even, while some even numbers, such as $2$, are not multiples of $4$. Thus $A\\subset B$, matching the diagram.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 16
      }
